    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\dfrac{(x+1)(x+2)}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are a lot of equations. For each one, use what you know about division to identify values of \\(x\\) that cannot be solutions to the equation.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Without solving, identify three of the equations that you think would be least difficult to solve and three that you think would be most difficult to solve. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Choose three equations to solve. At least one should be from your “least difficult” list and one should be from your “most difficult” list.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider the equation \\(\\frac{x+2}{x(x+1)} = \\frac{2}{(x+1)(x-1)}\\). We could solve this equation for \\(x\\) by multiplying each expression by \\(x(x+1)(x-1)\\) to get an equation with no variables in denominators, and then rearranging it…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} \\frac{x+2}{x(x+1)} \\boldcdot x(x+1)(x-1) &= \\frac{2}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\boldcdot x(x+1)(x-1) \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) &= 2x \\\\ x^2 + x - 2 &= 2x \\\\ x^2 - x - 2 &= 0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & = 0 \\\\ \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The last equation, \\((x-2)(x+1) = 0\\), leads us to believe that the original equation has two solutions: \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=\\text-1\\). Substituting \\(x=2\\) into the original equation, we get \\(\\frac{2+2}{2(2+1)} = \\frac{2}{(2+1)(2-1)}\\),…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let’s consider the simpler equation \\(x - 5 = 0\\). This equation has one solution, \\(x=5\\). But if we multiply each side by \\((x-1)\\) the result is a new equation, \\((x-1)(x-5)=0\\), which has solutions 5 and 1. The 1 is a solution to the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L22-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "22.3: More Rational Solving",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In the original example, \\(x=\\text-1\\) is the extraneous solution. While \\(x=\\text-1\\) is a solution to the equation we wrote after we multiplied the original equation by \\(x(x+1)(x-1)\\) on each side, it is not a solution to the original…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l22.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
